      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/uxm266/{size}/1966_2.png",
      "created_at": "2012-10-31T19:34:33.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"flamethrower49\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/f/838e76/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> flamethrower49:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>That sounds fun.&nbsp; I don't see why you wouldn't be able to do it for Redeemer Fanatic, Rook City Wraith, and Beard Ra, as well.&nbsp; You couldn't really do it as well with Legacy, considering that they are clearly, um, different people.&nbsp;There is the question about what you would do with the hit point totals.&nbsp; I think simply increasing and decreasing hit points by the proper amount every time the hero changes is the best solution.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>The reason i chose the freedom 6 only is because Tachyon - team leader is a different version of Tachyon (Normal Edition).</p>\n<p>RC Wraith, Ra, Fanatic are essentially come from the same universe as thier originals. ANd young legacy is a compeltely different person in the same universe. It can work, but the style i suggested caters to the different timeline.&nbsp;The freedom 6 are different universe-versions. Like parallel earths. Know what I mean?</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
